.TH LT-QWWAD_POPULATION_INIT "1" "September 2016" "lt-qwwad_population_init (Quantum Wells, Wires and Dots) 1.5.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lt-qwwad_population_init \- generate an appoximate carrier energy distribution.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lt-qwwad_population_init
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Generate an approximate carrier energy distribution for a set of subbands
.SH OPTIONS

Generic options:
.TP
\fB\-h\fR [ \fB\-\-help\fR ]
display a help message
.TP
\fB\-v\fR [ \fB\-\-version\fR ]
display the version of the program
.HP
\fB\-c\fR [ \fB\-\-config\fR ] arg (=qwwad.cfg) name of the configuration file to be used
.SS "Program-specific options:"
.TP
\fB\-\-dopingfile\fR arg (=d.r)
Filename from which doping profile is read
[m^{\-3}]
.TP
\fB\-\-energyfile\fR arg (=Ee.r)
Filename from which subband energies are read
[meV]
.TP
\fB\-\-mass\fR arg (=0.067)
In\-plane effective mass (relative to free
electron)
.TP
\fB\-\-Te\fR arg (=100)
Temperature of carrier distribution [K]
.TP
\fB\-\-populationfile\fR arg (=N.r) Set filename to which subband populations are
written [m^{\-2}]
.TP
\fB\-\-nval\fR arg (=1)
Split population between a number of equivalent
valleys
.TP
\fB\-\-type\fR arg (=even)
Type of carrier distribution across states.
Permitted options are: fermi, ground or even
.SS "Configuration options:"
.TP
\fB\-V\fR [ \fB\-\-verbose\fR ]
display lots of information about calculation
.SH FILES
.SS Input files:
   'Ee.r'    Energy of each state:
             Column 1: state index.
             Column 2: energy [meV].

   'd.r'     Doping profile:
             Column 1: spatial location [m]
             Column 2: volume doping [m^{-3}]

.SS Output files:

   'N.r' Populations of subbands:
         Column 1: Population [m^{-2}]

These filenames can be configured using command-line options.
.SH EXAMPLES
Put all carriers into the ground state
    qwwad_population_init --type ground

Generate a Fermi-Dirac distribution with carrier temperature = 200 K 
    qwwad_population_init --Te 200
.SH "DISTRIBUTION TYPES"
Three types of carrier distribution are available, which can be specified using the --type option.

.SS ground
With this option, all carriers are in the ground state.
This is a good approximation for very cold systems.
A negligible population (1 m^{-2}) is assigned to the higher states, since it is physically impossible to have a completely empty subband.

.SS even
With this option, each subband contains an identical population (i.e., carriers are distributed equally between states).
This is a reasonable approximation for e.g., periodic multi-quantum-well systems in an external electric field.

.SS fermi
With this option, carriers follow a thermalised Fermi-Dirac energy distribution.
The Fermi energy for the entire system is computed automatically, using the carrier temperature and effective mass specified by the --Te and --mass options.
